#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

bool isEven(int n){
    return !(n&1);
}
int main()
{
    int num;
    cin >> num;
    cout << boolalpha << isEven(num);
    
    
    return 0;
}


Note:
1. The Default Behavior

By default, `std::cout` treats booleans as integers:

• true -> 1

• false-> 0
ex: 
bool myBool = true;
cout << myBool;
output: 1

2. Using `std::boolalpha`

To print "true" or "false" as text, use the `boolalpha` manipulator from the `<iostream>` library.

• Syntax: `cout << boolalpha << myBool;`

• Note: This is "sticky"—it affects all subsequent boolean outputs in the stream until changed back using `noboolalpha`.

3. Custom Formatting (Capitalization)

Standard C++ does not have a built-in manipulator for "True" or "TRUE". To achieve this, use a Ternary Operator:

• Capitalized: `cout << (ans ? "True" : "False");`

• Upper Case: `cout << (ans ? "TRUE" : "FALSE");`
